{"records":[{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t2why6p3mg","title":"Two-color CRISPR imaging reveals dynamics of HSV-1 replication compartments and virus-host interactions","description":"This data submission contains the raw microscopy images  for our manuscript, which describes the following:  Real-time imaging tools for single-virus tracking provide spatially resolved, quantitative measurements of viral replication and virus-host interactions. However, efficiently labeling both parental and progeny viruses in living host cells remains challenging. Here, we have developed a novel strategy using the CRISPR-Tag system to detect herpes simplex virus 1 (HSV-1) DNA in host cells. We created recombinant HSV-1 harboring ~600 bp CRISPR-Tag sequence which can be sufficiently recognized by dCas9-fluorescent protein (FP) fusion proteins. CRISPR-assisted single viral genome tracking (CASVIT) allows us to assess the temporal and spatial information of viral replication at the single-cell level. Combining the advantages of SunTag and tandem split-GFP in amplifying fluorescent signals, dSaCas9-tdTomato10x and dSpCas9-GFP14x were constructed to enable efficient two-color CASVIT detection. Real-time two-color imaging indicates that replication compartments (RCs) frequently contact with each other but do not mix, suggesting that RC territory is highly stable. Lastly, two-color CASVIT enables simultaneous tracking of viral DNA and host chromatin, which reveals that a dramatic loss of telomeric and centromeric DNA occurs in host cells at the early stage of viral replication. Overall, our work has established a framework for developing CRISPR/Cas9-based imaging tools to study DNA viruses in living cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-24T07:17:10.187Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t2why6p3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t2why6p3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baohui","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dongli","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"txnvtdw2zs","title":"GPI-Anchored Ligand-BioID2-Tagging System Identifies Galectin-1 Mediating Zika Virus Entry Gao et al","description":"Identification of host factors facilitating pathogen entry is critical for preventing infectious diseases. Here, we report a tagging system consisting of a viral receptor-binding protein (RBP) linked to BioID2, which is expressed on the cell surface via a GPI anchor. Using VSV or Zika virus (ZIKV) RBP, the system (BioID2- RBP(V)-GPI; BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI) faithfully identifies LDLR and AXL, the receptors of VSV and ZIKV, respectively. Being GPI-anchored is essential for the probe to function properly. Furthermore, BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI expressed in human neuronal progenitor cells identifies galectin-1 on cell surface pivotal for ZIKV entry. This conclusion is further supported by antibody blocking and galectin-1 silencing in A549 and mouse neural cells. Importantly, Lgas1-/- mice are significantly more resistant to ZIKV infection than Lgas1+/+ littermates are, having significantly lower virus titers and fewer pathologies in various organs. This tagging system may have broad applications for identifying protein-protein interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T19:31:31.48Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/txnvtdw2zs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/txnvtdw2zs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shanshan","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gf3k5chdff","title":"Identification of host proteins differentially associated with HIV-1 RNA splice variants","description":"Western blots for siRNA knockdown confirmation.  FISH/IF co-localization of HIV-1 RNA and host proteins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-09T11:32:59.88Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gf3k5chdff","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gf3k5chdff"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"Knoener","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fluorescence Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7wfdbrfys","title":"HIV data for Livingstone district health facilities (2016)","description":"The human immune virus (HIV) is a viral infection that destroys the human immune system resulting in acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). If untreated, it can reduce the cluster of CD4 positive T-cells and increases the HIV viral load, thus causing AIDS. The Zambia HIV prevalence rate is among the highest in the sub-Saharan region. According to WHO, HIV/AIDS is a major cause of death in Zambia, with about a million deaths attributed to HIV/AIDS-related causes. With no HIV vaccine readily available and no permanent cure for HIV/AIDS, the antiretroviral (ARV) drug that slows the spread of the virus remains the only option. The ARV shuts down viral reproduction as well as reduces the immune suppression caused by HIV. Taking a combination of three ARV drugs from different classes suppresses the reproduction of the virus. The administration of ARV has challenges of Transmitted Drug Resistance Mutation strains (TDRMs) in the treatment of HIV naïve patients. In this article, we formulate a technique for determining an optimal ARV combination using Bayesian statistical methods. The proposed technique assist the medical personnel responsible in deciding the optimal ARV combination per patient in the presence of TDRMs test. We developed a transition probability matrix chart for each combination. Using the data from Zambia, we demonstrate the computation process and provide an interpretation of the obtained results. The findings from the analysis indicate that the probability of patients remaining on first baseline combinations namely, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 are: 0.96, 0.99, 0.97, 0.91, 0.96, and 0.96 respectively. The probabilities obtained can be \nused to choose an optimal ARV combination in the presence of Transmitted Drug Resistance Mutation Strains because you can isolate the particular drugs which the patient is resistance.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-15T19:23:55.109Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7wfdbrfys","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7wfdbrfys"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Urban Nchimunya","last_name":"Haankuku","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bayesian Statistics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pt5zkcd2wn","title":"Molecular analysis of multiple HIV-1 BC recombinant strains circulating worldwide shows predominance of the C genotype in the viral genomic structure","description":"Supplementary material of research entitled: Molecular analysis of multiple HIV-1 BC recombinant strains circulating worldwide shows predominance of the C genotype in the viral genomic structure.\n\nThe dataset contains 271 HIV-1 sequences, 225 belonging to the recombinant BC, plus 42 pure subtype reference sequences. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-10T13:10:54.13Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pt5zkcd2wn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pt5zkcd2wn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rodrigo","last_name":"Cunha Oliveira","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joana","last_name":"Monteiro-Cunha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Meta Dataset"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xykyffh2xv","title":"Host Transcriptional Corepressor TLE1 is commonly exploited by RNA viruses for replication","description":"This dataset delineates alterations in host genes transcriptionally regulated by Transducin-like enhancer of split 1 (TLE1) following influenza virus infection. TLE1, a transcriptional co-repressor encoded at chromosome 9q21.32, is clinically utilized as a diagnostic marker for synovial sarcoma. However, our analysis reveals that TLE1-regulated gene sets identified by ChIP enrichment are distinct post-infection, indicating its previously uncharacterized role in modulating transcriptional reprogramming during viral challenge. These findings provide novel insights for identifying host-directed antiviral targets and elucidate mechanisms by which influenza virus subverts host gene expression to facilitate replication and pathogenesis, thereby highlighting potential therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-17T13:54:13.077Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xykyffh2xv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xykyffh2xv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaomeng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haoran","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"kailai","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuofeng","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Influenza Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2m23dxy577","title":"Reconstruction of an intestinal epithelial–immune microenvironment enables trypsin-independent infection and sustained propagation of porcine epidemic diarrhea virus","description":"Porcine epidemic diarrhea virus (PEDV) is a highly contagious enteric coronavirus that causes severe diarrhea and high mortality in neonatal piglets. However, conventional in vitro systems have limited physiological relevance, and efficient infection and propagation of many PEDV strains require exogenous trypsin. An intestinal epithelial–immune microenvironment was reconstructed by integrating porcine intestinal organoids (PIOs) with intraepithelial lymphocytes (IELs). The PIO–IEL co-culture supported trypsin-independent infection and replication of cell-adapted PEDV strains, while the field-derived PEDV strain B18 was maintained through five serial passages without exogenous trypsin. Live-cell imaging revealed a temporal association between IEL accumulation around the organoids and PEDV enrichment at the epithelial surface, suggesting a potential role for IELs in facilitating viral access to the organoid epithelium. PEDV infection was accompanied by changes in adhesion, retention, and signaling molecules in the co-culture system, together with delayed antiviral and mucosal defense responses. The PIO–IEL co-culture may provide a useful system for PEDV maintenance and characterization and for investigating virus–epithelium–immune cell interactions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-04T17:35:46.978Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2m23dxy577","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2m23dxy577"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhifu","last_name":"Shan","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"U22A20527"}],"categories":["Intestinal Organoid"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":100193,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":132,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":100193,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":99885,"restricted":55,"embargoed":253},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1687},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":543},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":300},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":293},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":212}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":97825},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1767},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":170},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":94},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":60},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":58},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":50},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":33},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":27},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":426},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":378},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":331},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":319},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":258}]}}